olds).  ## Target Problems (in order) See `iterations.md`. Work on exactly ONE unchecked item per iteration.  ## Acceptance Criteria (per item) - [ ] Code lives under `learning/` with a module docstring saying what question it       answers and how to run it; runnable as `python -m learning.<name>`. - [ ] A section appended to `reports/ml_nature.md` (continuing loop0001's       numbering, §9 onwards) with: the question, the method, the baseline, the       table(s), the finding in one paragraph, the size range covered, the kill       criterion's verdict where the plan states one, and the regenerate command. - [ ] `python -m pytest tests/ -q -x` passes (~80 s). New code that computes a       quantity gets at least one test on a tiny hand-checkable instance. - [ ] The item is marked `- [x]` (done) or `- [!]` (blocked, with a reason in       PROGRESS.md) in `iterations.md`. - [ ] No regressions, no changes to solver defaults, nothing written to       `solutions/`. Generated instances and their witnesses go under       `learning/data/ensemble/` (see Important Notes on persistence).  ## Completion Conditions An item is DONE when either: 1. **Solved**: meets all criteria and the report section is written, OR 2. **Blocked**: documented in PROGRESS.md with the specific blocker.  ## Context - **Key files**: `benchmarks/generator.py` (`generate_random_instance` with   Bernoulli `p`; `generate_structured_instance` with Chu & Stuckey's fixed   customers-per-product `d`, which loop0001 §2 showed is what their density   class means), `learning/node_counts.py` (`refute`, the per-call primitive;   `refutation_kwargs` for the two configurations), `learning/canonical.py`   (certificates for deduplication), `learning/features.py` (49 features   including the §4 invariants; `tw_min_fill`, `bw_rcm`, `g_degeneracy`),   `learning/formula_search.py` (the enumerated search and the sandwich table,   reusable for `E[opt](n, m, p)`), `learning/degeneracy.py` (`count_search`   is a graph invariant, `count_closing` is not: a §2.9 quantity),   `learning/bound_gap.py` (`treewidth_upper_bound`, `describe_instance`),   `mosp/preprocess.py` (component decomposition — record `g_components`, do   not discard decomposable instances silently; Chu & Stuckey did, and say so),   `benchmarks/results/compute_ledger.csv` and `recertify/results.json` (the   125×125 node counts for item 06). - **Data**: `solutions/*.json` — 6,376 certified optima (read-only here);   `learning/data/canonical.csv`, `instances.csv`, `node_counts.csv` from loop0001. - **Testing**: `python -m pytest tests/ -q -x`. - **Dependencies**: `learning/requirements.txt`; new packages only behind soft   imports; the core package must import without them.  ## Important Notes - Do NOT run `benchmarks.csearch`, `benchmarks.recertify`, `benchmarks.overnight`   or any sweep that writes to `solutions/`. Generated instances are solved with   `solve_mosp_exact(..., solutions_dir=<learning/data/ensemble/solutions>)`,   never `solutions_dir=None` and never the corpus directory. - **Persistence**: `learning/data/` is git-ignored. The campaign's instance   files, witnesses and the results table are the artifact of this loop and cost   hours to regenerate, so item 01 adds a negation rule to `.gitignore` for   `learning/data/ensemble/` (instances as `.mosp`, witnesses as JSON, results   as CSV) and they are committed by the driver with the code. Keep the total   under ~50 MB; if the grid is larger, commit the results CSV and a manifest of   seeds from which the instances regenerate byte for byte. - Do NOT edit `CLAUDE.md`; the loop's owner will fold findings in afterwards. - Do NOT rewrite existing report sections; append. If a finding contradicts an   earlier one, say so in the new section and leave the old one in place. - Commit nothing yourself; the driver commits after tests pass. - You are running in print mode: **ending your turn ends the session.** Never   end a turn waiting on a background job or a wait/monitor